Moth checks whether the source and target types support a conversion before
lowering the program. Builtin types have a fixed set of conversions. A
struct or choice can supply its own conversion to one of the supported
targets by declaring conformance to a core cast trait.

### Builtin evidence

The complete pair of types determines whether a builtin conversion can
fail. For example, `U16` to `U8` can fail even when the compiler knows
the value fits. It can omit a redundant range check without changing the
conversion's contract. Builtin conversions also cover `Byte` and the
`Dec` family. See @./#cast-targets (Cast targets).

### Source-authored evidence

A same-file struct or choice can supply an infallible or fallible method.
A generic struct or choice can declare the conversion for the whole
constructor. The method needs explicit conformance. An infallible method
must handle any inferred failure before it escapes. A fallible method
declares a builtin `Error!` slot.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    Digits = | text String |

    try_to_i32 |this Digits| -> I32, Error! {
        return cast this.text
    }

    Digits must TRY_CASTABLE_TO_I32

    count I32 = cast Digits("42") catch then 0
]

The return type of `try_to_i32` sets the target for the builtin cast of
the text. If parsing fails, that implicit failure can leave the method
through its declared `Error!` slot. The outer cast uses the method and
its caller can recover with an ordinary `catch`.

@../traits/#core-cast-traits (Core cast traits) lists all fifteen targets
and the required method signatures. The compiler owns those trait names.
Use them without an import. A type can claim only one of the two traits
for a given target. The traits provide source conversions to those
targets only.

### Outside cast design scope

- user-defined target types
- generic cast target parameters
- external opaque cast targets
- generic cast traits
- broad return-type-directed conversion
- conversion search through an intermediate target
- implicit conversion selected from distant context

See @./#cast-targets (Cast targets) for builtin conversion exactness and
@../traits/#trait-incompatibility (Trait incompatibility) for conflicting
trait implementations.
